  "status": null,
  "background": [
    {
      "text": "Incapacitation is a real safety hazard that occurs more frequently than many other emergencies. It ranges from sudden death to partial loss of function, sometimes without any symptom beforehand.",
      "applies_to": [
        "all"
      ],
      "refs": [
        {
          "book": "FCTM",
          "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
          "page": "P 9/32",
          "pdf": 454
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Definition: any condition which affects the health of a crew member during the performance of duties which renders him incapable of performing the assigned duties.",
      "applies_to": [
        "all"
      ],
      "refs": [
        {
          "book": "PDP",
          "code": "Crew incapacitation",
          "page": "XFTDA1INCAPAC01",
          "pdf": 517
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Pressing the sidestick pb gives priority to that sidestick: the aural \"PRIORITY LEFT\" sounds on a captain takeover, and the other sidestick is inoperative.",
      "applies_to": [
        "all"
      ],
      "refs": [
        {
          "book": "PDP",
          "code": "Crew incapacitation",
          "page": "XFTDA1INCAPAC01",
          "pdf": 520
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "technique": [
    {
      "text": "The decision to reject the takeoff and the stop action are the responsibility of the Captain. The \"STOP\" call both confirms the decision and states that the Captain now has control; it is the only hand-over of control not accompanied by \"I have control\".",
      "applies_to": [
        "all"
      ],
      "refs": [
        {
          "book": "FCTM",
          "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
          "page": "P 19/32",
          "pdf": 464
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "related": [
    {
      "title": "Emergency evacuation",
      "why": "If an evacuation becomes necessary on the ground.",
      "key_actions": [
        "See the Emergency evacuation brief"
      ],
      "refs": [
        {
          "book": "FCTM",
          "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
          "page": "P 21/32",
          "pdf": 466
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": [
    {
      "text": "The manuals have no specific procedure for incapacitation during a rejected takeoff, including who continues the stop if the Captain is the one incapacitated. The brief follows the general rule that the fit pilot takes over, confirmed by the user.",
      "applies_to": [
        "all"
      ],
      "refs": [
        {
          "book": "FCTM",
          "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
          "page": "P 10/32",
          "pdf": 455
        },
        {
          "book": "FCTM",
          "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
          "page": "P 19/32",
          "pdf": 464
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The PA wording differs: FCOM \"PURSER TO COCKPIT, PLEASE!\", PDP \"ATTENTION, PURSER TO COCKPIT PLEASE\". The brief uses the FCOM wording, confirmed by the user.",
      "applies_to": [
        "all"
      ],
      "refs": [
        {
          "book": "FCOM",
          "code": "PRO-ABN-ABN-00",
          "page": "P 9/12",
          "pdf": 2832
        },
        {
          "book": "PDP",
          "code": "Crew incapacitation",
          "page": "XFTDA1INCAPAC01",
          "pdf": 519
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The FCTM says press the sidestick pb \"for 40 seconds\"; the PDP says \"at least 40 seconds\". The brief uses the FCTM wording, confirmed by the user.",
      "applies_to": [
        "all"
      ],
      "refs": [
        {
          "book": "FCTM",
          "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
          "page": "P 10/32",
          "pdf": 455
        },
        {
          "book": "PDP",
